 5. 🇮🇱 Netanyahu gets Trump endorsement 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump waves as he walks past presidential portraits along the Colonnade before departing the White House on Friday. Photo: Jacquelyn Martin/AP President Trump endorsed Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu yesterday, just nine days before Israel's Oct. 27 elections.

 Why it matters: This is the first time a U.S. president explicitly and publicly endorsed a candidate in Israeli elections, Axios' Barak Ravid writes. 

 It could give Netanyahu a boost at a critical moment in the Israeli leader's fight for political survival. Most polls show his coalition falling short of the 61 seats needed to form a government. In yesterday's post on Truth Social , Trump called Netanyahu "a Great Warrior Prime Minister" and a "great partner" in the war against Iran.

 Trump had repeatedly declined to endorse Netanyahu in recent months. Some of Trump's closest advisers believed his Middle East agenda would fare better if Netanyahu lost and a different government formed. 📱 Trump told Barak in a short phone interview: "I was always going to do that. I just didn't want to do it too soon."

